{{Documentation/Header}}
<!-- Template Description -->
Returns a noun describing the type of the current page — normally '''article''', but '''page''', '''file''', '''template''', '''category''' or '''book''' depending on the namespace. Intended for use ''inside'' other templates (hatnotes, maintenance notices) so their wording adapts automatically.

By default it inspects the page it sits on. You can override the result or point it at another page:
* <code><nowiki>{{Pagetype|book}}</nowiki></code> — force the "book" wording
* <code><nowiki>{{Pagetype|page=Mafumafu}}</nowiki></code> — evaluate a named page
* <code><nowiki>{{Pagetype|subjectspace=yes}}</nowiki></code> — use the subject page when called from a talk page

==Syntax==
{{T|Pagetype|v1=|v2=page=|v3=subjectspace=}}

== Parameters ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Parameter !! Description !! Default
|-
| <code>1</code> || Override / hint word: <code>book</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>template</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>portal</code>, <code>disambig</code>, <code>redirect</code>, <code>na</code>… || derived from namespace
|-
| <code>page</code> || Evaluate this page title instead of the current page || current page
|-
| <code>subjectspace</code> || Set to <code>yes</code> to use the subject page when on a talk page || —
|}

==Examples==
{| class="wikitable"
! Code !! Result
|-
| <code><nowiki>{{Pagetype}}</nowiki></code> (on an article) || article
|-
| <code><nowiki>{{Pagetype|page=Mafumafu}}</nowiki></code> || {{Pagetype|page=Mafumafu}}
|-
| <code><nowiki>{{Pagetype|category}}</nowiki></code> || {{Pagetype|category}}
|}

